    "created_at": "2026-05-04T13:13:52Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-05-04T13:13:52Z",
    "body": "> Mozilla implemented the original name of [HTMLOrForeignElement](https://searchfox.org/firefox-main/source/dom/webidl/Element.webidl#175) and included it in [MathMLElement](https://searchfox.org/firefox-main/source/dom/webidl/MathMLElement.webidl#15)\r\n> \r\n> WebKit seems to have done the same, and I believe chromium were supportive before so I think we can consider this to have implementor interest.\r\n\r\nFWIW Chromium has this too:\r\nhttps://source.chromium.org/chromium/chromium/src/+/main:third_party/blink/renderer/core/html/html_or_foreign_element.idl;l=8;drc=047c7dc4ee1ce908d7fea38ca063fa2f80f92c77\r\nhttps://source.chromium.org/chromium/chromium/src/+/main:third_party/blink/renderer/core/mathml/mathml_element.idl\r\n\r\nIn any case, I believe this name is not exposed to Web developers.\r\n\r\n> If there's a lack of consensus on MathML <a> specifically I'm happy to drop that reference from this PR and wait till it's further specified and prototyped before including that change.\r\n\r\nJust some more context on this.\r\n\r\nHistorically in MathML3 the `href` attribute was supported on all elements but there were [safety concerns about it](https://github.com/w3c/mathml-core/issues/142). At the end, the consensus seems to be that we want to introduce an `<a>` as a single linkable tag, similar to SVG/HTML, which would have similar layout as `<mrow>`.\r\n\r\nMathML Core says any unknown MathML element should be laid out as as `<mrow>`. Chromium's MathML Core implementation has always had this behavior, and it was implemented some months ago in [Firefox](https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=1958853) and [WebKit](https://bugs.webkit.org/show_bug.cgi?id=290625). This means `<a>` is already laid out that way in any case.\r\n\r\nAdditionally, Firefox and WebKit's implementation of the `href` attribute is still working for `<a>` and also being deprecated on other elements, see https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=2026848 and https://github.com/WebKit/WebKit/pull/61628\r\n\r\nChromium's initial implementation of MathML Core does not support links, essentially because we couldn't find a consensus on the issue I mentioned above. But now that we agreed on having a single linkable element I personally think it makes sense to implement it. I don't think there is an official position from the Chromium project on that though.",
